 Today's newsletter is 743 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📉 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down 0.2%.

 ⚠️ Retail sales fell a surprise 0.6% in July, the weakest performance in more than a year. The S&amp;P finished the week up 0.4% 📈. It's up 13.7% for the year 📈. 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: PayPal (+1.8%) jumped in the last hour of trading after WSJ reported that the company is in talks to sell itself to a group that includes Stripe and private equity firm Advent International.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Drone protection 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The stocks of American drone companies got a sudden lift today after President Trump announced plans to impose tariffs on imported drones.

 Unusual Machines, which was backed by Donald Trump Jr. in 2024 when he joined its advisory board, closed up 24%. Red Cat Holdings, which pivoted from hobby drones to military ones, finished up nearly 9%. Ondas, AeroVironment and Kratos Defense &amp; Security Solutions gained 3.7%, 1.8% and 2.9%, respectively. The big picture: National security experts have long bemoaned the American reliance on foreign sources for drone production, particularly China.

 "President Trump's drone tariff program will protect the national security of the United States and its defense and defense-adjacent industrial base, supporting and creating American jobs," the White House said . Zoom in: Trump announced multiple different buckets of drone tariffs:

 100% on large drones with "capabilities that are particularly sensitive for national security purposes," including thermal imaging. 25% on drone parts and smaller drones "that lack certain capabilities that particularly implicate national security." 15% on drones and parts from the European Union and several other key allies; and 10% on units from the U.K. 💭 Our thought bubble via Axios Future of Defense author Colin Demarest : This deepens the U.S.-China rivalry at a time when sketchy weapon parts and complicated supply chains are top of mind.

